## Solution

The region $R$ is defined by $x^2 + 4y^2 \ge 1$ and $x^2 + y^2 \le 1$. The ellipse $x^2+4y^2=1$ has semi-axes $1$ (in $x$) and $\tfrac12$ (in $y$), so it lies entirely inside the unit circle, touching it only at $(\pm1,0)$. Hence

$$R = \{(x,y): x^2+y^2\le 1\} \setminus \{(x,y): x^2+4y^2<1\}.$$

By the additivity of the integral and the fact that $|xy|\ge 0$:

$$I = \iint_{x^2+y^2\le 1}|xy|\,dx\,dy \;-\; \iint_{x^2+4y^2\le 1}|xy|\,dx\,dy.$$

### Integral over the unit disk

By the four-fold symmetry of $|xy|$ about both axes:

$$\iint_{x^2+y^2\le 1}|xy|\,dx\,dy = 4\int_0^{\pi/2}\int_0^1 (r\cos\theta)(r\sin\theta)\,r\,dr\,d\theta.$$

Separating:

$$= 4\left(\int_0^{\pi/2}\cos\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta\right)\left(\int_0^1 r^3\,dr\right) = 4\cdot\frac12\cdot\frac14 = \frac12.$$

### Integral over the ellipse

Use the change of variables $u=x,\; v=2y$, so $dx\,dy = \tfrac12\,du\,dv$ and the ellipse maps to the unit disk $u^2+v^2\le 1$. Since $|xy|=\tfrac12|uv|$,

$$\iint_{x^2+4y^2\le 1}|xy|\,dx\,dy = \iint_{u^2+v^2\le 1}\frac{|uv|}{2}\cdot\frac12\,du\,dv = \frac14\iint_{u^2+v^2\le 1}|uv|\,du\,dv = \frac14\cdot\frac12 = \frac18.$$

### Result

$$I = \frac12 - \frac18 = \frac38.$$

